-- **How many pass slots does a value-yielding, breakable search loop open?**
--
-- «B10» asks for a loop that finds a thing and stops. `K001` killed the previous attempt at
-- a construct because it needed more than one pass slot, so the question is measured BEFORE
-- anything is built. This file is a MEASUREMENT and not an example; it runs against the
-- UNCHANGED checker.
--
-- Six questions, one function each. What the checker says about each is recorded under
-- `1. AGAINST THE UNCHANGED CHECKER` below -- literally, before anything was repaired.
--
--   (a) a binding without an initialiser -- the shape a not-found path would create
--   (b) the sentinel workaround, written correctly (this is the whole corpus today)
--   (c) the same, with the guard MISSING -- `messung/netz/udp-echo.gab`:167 shipped this
--   (d) does the cost pass charge the body once per domain element?
--   (e) may the loop WRITE the element it finds? (caprock `heap.rs`:112 does)
--   (f) `by consuming` -- the F03 shape: the walk empties the whole queue
--
-- 1. AGAINST THE UNCHANGED CHECKER, 2026-09-03
-- ---------------------------------------------
--
--     $ gabbro pruefe messung/proben/probe-suchschleife-passfach.gab
--
-- (a) is a PARSE refusal and not a pass refusal:
--
--     error: [P001] …:NN:26: `=` expected, `;` found
--
-- **That is the finding.** `letstmt` (`SYNTAX.md`:961) has no form without `=`, so an
-- uninitialised binding does not exist in this language -- not as an error, as an
-- unwritable shape. There is therefore **no definite-assignment pass today, because there
-- is nothing for one to do.** A construct that yields a value out of a loop would create
-- the first binding in the language whose value depends on a path -- and that is the pass
-- slot, spelled out.
--
-- (b) through (e): 0 errors.
--
-- (d) was measured by tightening the promise until `K001` fires, at two domain sizes:
--
--     NSLOTS=16   costs <= 48 -> refused   costs <= 64 -> accepted
--     NSLOTS=32   costs <= 96 -> refused   costs <= 128 -> accepted
--
-- **Four ops per element, and it doubles with the domain.** The cost pass charges the body
-- once per domain element, so **an early exit costs the cost pass nothing**: the worst case
-- of a loop that may stop early is the loop that does not, and that is the promise already
-- written. The rest of the record stands in `messung/BERICHT-SUCHE.md`.
--
-- 2. WHAT THE CORPUS DOES INSTEAD -- measured, not assumed
-- ---------------------------------------------------------
-- 57 loops in the 179 corpus files: 33 `traverse`, 13 `retry`, 11 `forever`.
-- **`leave` already works on 24 of them** (every `retry` and `forever` carries a label, 15
-- exit statements use it). The gap is `traverse` alone: it carries no label, so 10 of its
-- 33 sites carry the value out by hand -- 8 with a `return` out of the whole function, 2
-- with a sentinel accumulator. **One of those two is wrong** -- see (c).

module probe::suchschleifepassfach {

const NSLOTS : u32 = 16;

table Tafel count NSLOTS {
    slot {
        belegt : bool,
        schluessel : u32,
        wert : u32,
    }
}

-- (a) **A binding without an initialiser** -- the shape the not-found path of a yielding
--     loop would have to create. Refused by the PARSER, so no pass ever sees it.
--     *Uncomment to reproduce the `P001` above; it is left commented so that the rest of
--     this file measures.*
--
--     impl fn ohne_vorbelegung(a : ptr<normal, r> Tafel) -> u32
--         effects { reads a.slots }
--         costs   <= 512 ops
--     {
--         let mut treffer : u32;
--         traverse s over slots of a by unvisited touches reads a.slots {
--             if a.slots[s].belegt { treffer = a.slots[s].wert; }
--         }
--         return treffer;
--     }

-- (b) **The sentinel workaround, written correctly.** This is what every yielding site in
--     the corpus does today. The guard `treffer == 0 &&` is the hand-built `break`: it does
--     not stop the walk, it stops the ASSIGNMENT, and the walk pays for the whole domain
--     either way.
impl fn erster_treffer(a : ptr<normal, r> Tafel, schluessel : u32) -> u32
    effects { reads a.slots }
    costs   <= 512 ops
{
    let mut treffer : u32 = 0;
    traverse s over slots of a by unvisited
        touches reads a.slots
    {
        if treffer == 0 && a.slots[s].belegt && a.slots[s].schluessel == schluessel {
            treffer = a.slots[s].wert;
        }
    }
    return treffer;
}

-- (c) **The same function with the guard MISSING.** It returns the LAST match, not the
--     first. `gabbro pruefe` says nothing about it -- no error, no hint -- and neither does
--     any annotation this language can write. **`messung/netz/udp-echo.gab`:167 carried
--     exactly this until 2026-09-03**, under the name `arp_suchen` and documented as a
--     search. It was repaired the same day -- with `return` in the body, which needs no
--     construct and is what four other corpus sites already do.
impl fn letzter_treffer(a : ptr<normal, r> Tafel, schluessel : u32) -> u32
    effects { reads a.slots }
    costs   <= 512 ops
{
    let mut treffer : u32 = 0;
    traverse s over slots of a by unvisited
        touches reads a.slots
    {
        if a.slots[s].belegt && a.slots[s].schluessel == schluessel {
            treffer = a.slots[s].wert;
        }
    }
    return treffer;
}

-- (d) **Does the cost pass charge the body once per domain element?** The promise here is
--     the smallest one that still passes; one step lower and `K001` refuses. That number is
--     the answer, and it is why an early exit costs the cost pass NOTHING: the worst case of
--     a loop that may stop early is the loop that does not, and that is the promise already
--     written.
impl fn kostenprobe(a : ptr<normal, r> Tafel) -> u32
    effects { reads a.slots }
    costs   <= 64 ops
{
    let mut summe : u32 in 0 .. 65535 = 0;
    traverse s over slots of a by unvisited
        touches reads a.slots
        invariant summe <= 65535
    {
        if a.slots[s].belegt {
            summe += 1;
        }
    }
    return summe;
}

-- (e) **May the loop WRITE the element it finds?** It may -- today, without any construct.
--     `../caprock-messbasis/crates/caprock-region/src/heap.rs`:112 sets `slot.bump` before
--     returning the slot's address, and `caprock-hal/src/x86_64/pcie.rs`:169 writes the
--     bus-master bit before returning the device. **A construct that yields the hit but
--     forbids the write does not serve those sites** -- and this function is the proof that
--     the write itself is not the missing part.
impl fn treffer_aendern(a : ptr<normal, rw> Tafel, schluessel : u32) -> u32
    effects { reads a.slots, writes a.slots }
    costs   <= 512 ops
{
    let mut treffer : u32 = 0;
    traverse s over slots of a by unvisited
        touches reads a.slots, writes a.slots
    {
        if treffer == 0 && a.slots[s].belegt && a.slots[s].schluessel == schluessel {
            a.slots[s].belegt = false;
            treffer = a.slots[s].wert;
        }
    }
    return treffer;
}

}
